# =========================================================== 1 Introduction
para('Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) suffers from low electrification rates',
r"""Electrification rates in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) remain low \cite{SSA_decent}. In 2023, 666 million people, about 8\% of the world's population, still lacked access to electricity, and 565 million of them lived in SSA \cite{sdg7_2025}. Up to 85\% of the unserved population lives in remote rural villages far from national grids, and many of the remaining 15\%, who live closer to a grid, cannot afford the connection, metering and wiring costs \cite{quak_CBA}. Electricity access is closely tied to living standards, productivity, employment and health, so improving it supports poverty alleviation and several of the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) \cite{SSA_decent}.""")
para('It is challenging to connect such isolated communities',
r"""Connecting isolated communities to the grid is expensive. According to Power for All, grid extension and infrastructure upgrades in sub-Saharan Africa can cost up to \$5.5 million per MW/km \cite{grid_gtm}, and long planning, procurement, environmental assessment and construction phases lead to budget overruns and delays. Connection fees average about \$136 for a single-phase connection and can exceed monthly household incomes, so extension rarely pays in sparsely populated rural areas with low, dispersed demand. Even households with access often spend a large share of their income on electricity and fuel. Close to half of the world's population still relies on firewood or charcoal for cooking and heating and on kerosene for lighting, which are expensive and harm health, and almost two-thirds of the world's poorest people live in rural areas \cite{kammen}. Rural electrification therefore needs affordable, reliable options that can be deployed quickly.""")
block(r"Microgrids are low-voltage, localized hubs of electricity generation",
      r"This choice introduces some additional special challenges that will be discussed in greater detail in \cref{sec:MG_challenges}. ",
r"""Microgrids are low-voltage, local systems of generation, storage and demand. A grid-connected microgrid exchanges power with the main grid, within transmission limits, and can disconnect at the point of common coupling to operate on its own; its operator can also sell surplus renewable power to the grid. An islanded microgrid has no such connection: it must meet all demand locally and curtail any surplus through dump loads. The growth of distributed generation from renewable sources (RES) such as wind, solar, geothermal, biomass and small hydro has made both types more practical. Because grid extension is costly (\cref{sec:access}), this work considers only stand-alone systems that always operate in islanded mode, which adds the challenges discussed in \cref{sec:MG_challenges}.""")
para('In comparison to large-scale conventional sources such as thermal and nuclear baseload',
r"""Microgrids are much smaller than thermal and nuclear baseload plants but more modular and flexible, which eases the integration of variable distributed energy resources (DERs) that are often located far from load centers. Feeding variable renewables into a conventional grid can degrade power quality through unsteady voltages and harmonics, and large interconnected grids are vulnerable to natural disasters, extreme weather and cyber-physical attacks: a fault in one section can cascade, as the blackouts, brownouts and load-shedding common in urban areas of developing nations show \cite{macrogrid_prob}.""")
para('By reducing our reliance on fossil fuels, microgrids',
r"""By displacing fossil fuels, microgrids can lower greenhouse gas emissions and support the SDGs and the Paris Agreement. Because they can island during faults and emergencies, they keep supplying local load and make the wider network more resilient, and controllable microgrids can relieve congestion and provide ancillary services to the main grid, such as load shifting, frequency or voltage control and peak shaving. Generating close to demand also reduces transmission and distribution (T\&D) losses and defers T\&D investment. Microgrids are among the few realistic options for low-cost local supply at other remote, resource-constrained sites, such as islands, military bases and Arctic settlements. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that about 70\% of the rural areas that lack access will be connected through mini-grid, microgrid or off-grid solutions \cite{WEO_2018,mentis}, which could bring electricity to about 140 million rural Africans through 100,000 mini-grids by 2040 \cite{wri}.""")
para('Despite the numerous merits of microgrids, they still face',
r"""Microgrids also face practical challenges. Being small, they lack the averaging that large networks gain from transmission spanning regions with different weather and load patterns, so their load fluctuations are relatively larger, and unfavorable weather, such as extended wind lulls or cloudy and rainy days, can leave load unmet. To cover worst cases, designers often oversize renewable and storage capacity, which raises project cost.""")
para('These issues are further intensified in the case of isolated microgrids',
r"""Isolated microgrids have no main grid, with its peaker plants and interconnectors, to fall back on, and they lack large synchronous generators whose inertia resists sudden changes in frequency and voltage. Mismatches between supply and demand of real and reactive power must therefore be corrected quickly to keep frequency and voltage stable \cite{tech_challenges}. This study addresses power balancing on time scales of hours to months. Frequency and voltage stability act over seconds to minutes and depend mainly on switchgear and power-electronic design, so they are outside its scope.""")

# =========================================================== 2 Literature review
para('There is a large body of existing research in the field of microgrid design and operation.',
r"""Research on microgrids ranges from the selection and sizing of generation and storage technologies to the real-time energy management and dispatch of these components.""")
para('Once the most suitable technology types are selected',
r"""Once the technologies have been chosen on economic, technical and socio-political grounds, sizing sets the rating and number of units of each renewable source (for example, the number of wind turbines (WT) or the area of the solar photovoltaic (PV) array), the power rating and energy capacity of storage, and the rated power of backup diesel engines or gas turbines. Most studies of stand-alone systems consider \textit{hybrid} designs that combine two or more renewable sources with fossil-fueled distributed generators (DGs) as backup to meet reliability requirements \cite{kaabeche2014} \cite{bhandari2014}. They minimize economic cost \cite{tazvinga2013}, pollutant emissions and measures of power quality, reliability and efficiency \cite{kaabeche2011a}, which reflect the goals of the community served. \cref{app:costs} details the individual cost terms.""")
para('Past studies have shed interesting insights into trade-offs',
r"""Past studies show the trade-offs involved. PV/WT systems that use both diesel generators and batteries are more economical than those that use only batteries or only diesel engines, and in a region of Algeria with a strong solar resource, configurations with far fewer wind turbines than PV modules had the lowest energy cost and net CO$_2$ emissions \cite{kaabeche2014}; Timbila, Kenya, the site studied here, also has a strong solar resource. Sizing results therefore depend on the local climate, the wind and solar resources and the demand profile, and also on the objectives and on financial parameters such as interest, discount and inflation rates, because capital dominates the cost of renewables and storage while fuel dominates that of backup generation. The objectives often compete, since more reliable and cleaner systems usually cost more; matching the reliability of the main grid can make off-grid PV--battery systems up to ten times more expensive than on-grid solutions \cite{weng2016}.""")
para('Overall mathematical models for hybrid microgrids are constructed',
r"""Models of hybrid microgrids combine well-established models of the wind, solar, diesel, gas and battery subsystems \cite{bhandari2014}. The sizing of storage and backup generation often determines system performance: the efficiency of microgrid operation depends strongly on battery scheduling, because the battery shifts peak load and smooths fluctuations in demand and renewable supply \cite{gu2014}. Most studies that build their own models simulate one year (8,760~h) \cite{logenthiran2010}, while commercial and open-source design tools can simulate longer periods; \cref{app:tools} reviews the main tools.""")
block(r"This area of research studies methods for both:", r"\end{itemize}",
r"""Operational studies address unit commitment, which decides which dispatchable units (generators and storage) are online in each period to meet forecast demand at minimum cost \cite{saravanan2013}, and unit dispatch, which sets the output of each online unit in each interval.""")
para('Whereas single-objective optimizations generally minimize purely economic costs',
r"""Single-objective formulations usually minimize cost, but multiobjective formulations that also cover emissions, power loss and heat are increasingly common \cite{pathak2017}. Combined economic and emissions dispatch problems are generally nonlinear, non-smooth and non-convex, with demanding equality and inequality constraints, which makes conventional optimization methods ill-suited and raises the risk of convergence to local minima \cite{pathak2017}. As mixed-integer problems they combine discrete and continuous decision variables in high dimension, which rules out disciplined convex programming tools such as CVX and has motivated metaheuristic approaches. Some studies replace hard constraints by heavily penalized soft constraints to keep the problem feasible \cite{zachar2016}.""")
para('Some focus areas of past microgrid operations research include',
r"""Other operational research covers seamless islanding and reconnection to the grid \cite{mg_review}, primary and secondary control of frequency and voltage, and the third, tertiary level of control for economic optimization that microgrids need but large grids do not \cite{mg_review}. Microgrid modeling can also borrow from related applications: electric vehicles as controllable loads, weather-dependent generation such as solar PV, controllable generation and storage models \cite{mg_mod_opt}. Some studies simulate simplified dynamic models of small microgrids in MATLAB \texttt{Simulink}.""")
para('In addition to geographical and spatial factors, temporal variations are critical',
r"""Temporal patterns in load and renewable supply, both seasonal and monthly as well as daily and hourly, determine how the battery cycles and how long the backup DG runs, and hence fuel use and operating cost \cite{tazvinga2013}. Optimal configuration and operation improve power quality, reliability, security and sustainability and lower the cost to consumers \cite{pathak2017}. For islanded microgrids without grid support, incentive-based demand response (DR) \cite{nwulu2017} and model predictive control \cite{zachar2016} help reject forecast errors and noise in load and renewable output. Game-theoretic work on demand-side management (DSM) shows that price signals that lead individual agents to minimize their own costs can produce globally optimal aggregate demand profiles \cite{paola2017}. Integrated design and operation optimization, which sizes and dispatches the system in a single stage rather than in two sequential steps, is another recent direction \cite{moshi2016}.""")

# =========================================================== 3 System description
block(r"The microgrid consists of the following key components as shown in \cref{fig:chap3_MG}:",
      r"Detailed physical models of all elements in the MG can be found in \cref{app:mg_models}, including information about climate data inputs (for a specific location in Kenya) and load characteristics. ",
r"""The microgrid (\cref{fig:chap3_MG}) consists of:

\begin{itemize}
    \item non-dispatchable renewable sources (RES): solar photovoltaic arrays (PV) and wind turbines (WT);
    \item dispatchable fossil-fueled distributed generators (DG) for backup: a diesel engine (DE) or a microturbine (MT);
    \item battery storage (BS): lithium-ion (LI) or lead-acid (LA);
    \item charge controllers that regulate the battery's state of charge (SOC), charging and discharging;
    \item power conditioners that regulate the power delivered from the RES to the DC bus;
    \item bidirectional converters that act as an inverter (DC to AC) or a rectifier (AC to DC).
\end{itemize}

The system is a hybrid, islanded microgrid that serves a remote rural community: it can neither buy power from nor sell power to an external grid. \cref{app:mg_models} gives the component models, the climate data for the site in Kenya and the load characteristics.""")

# =========================================================== 4 Formulation
block(r"A discrete-time, iterative optimization is performed involving two components:",
      r"Further details on all costs and objective functions can be found in \cref{app:costs}. ",
r"""The two stages are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Design and sizing (Stage 1)} over one year (8,760~h) in hourly steps ($\Delta t = 1$~h). The decision variables are the number of PV modules $n_s$, the number of wind turbines $n_w$ and the initial rated battery capacity $E_{b,\;init}$. Inside the optimizer, the annual simulation dispatches the units by a priority rule (\cref{sec:disp_strat}) rather than by an inner optimization, which keeps each evaluation cheap: one evaluation of the 8,760-hour simulation takes about 2\,ms on a current laptop, so a sizing run of 2,000 evaluations completes in seconds (\cref{sec:results}).
    \item \textbf{Day-ahead dispatch (Stage 2)}, an economic and environmental dispatch (EED) over 24~h in hourly steps. With the capacities fixed by Stage 1, it optimizes the DG output $P_{DG}(t)$ and the battery power $P_{BS}(t)$; the renewable output $P_{RES}$ follows the weather and is not dispatchable.
\end{enumerate}

The problem has five objectives: (i) levelized cost of electricity (LCOE), (ii) emissions (Em), (iii) deficiency of power supply probability (DPSP), (iv) relative excess or dump power generated (REPG) and (v) renewable energy fraction (REF). This section states the optimization problems and their constraints; \cref{app:costs} defines the costs and objectives.""")
block(r"For the sizing and design optimization, a priority order for scheduling",
      r"\label{tab:dispatch_strat_for_sizing} ",
r"""In the sizing problem, a priority order for the generation and storage units is encoded in the objective function. \cref{tab:dispatch_strat_for_sizing} lists this load-following rule.
\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{0.75cm} p{13cm}}
\toprule
\multicolumn{1}{c}{\textbf{Step}} & \multicolumn{1}{c}{\textbf{Action}}    \\
\midrule
1 & Use the RES output to meet the load. \\ [1ex]
2 & If the RES output (PV and WT) exceeds the load, charge the battery with the surplus. \\ [1ex]
3 & If the surplus exceeds what the battery can absorb (its power rating or the headroom below $SOC_{max}$), send the remainder to the dump load. \\ [1ex]
4 & If the RES output falls short of the load, discharge the battery. \\ [1ex]
5 & If the battery reaches $SOC_{min}$ or its power rating and load remains unmet, dispatch the backup DG until all demand is met or the DG reaches its rated power. \\ [1ex]
6 & The DG cannot run below its minimum stable output $P_{DG,\;min}$. If the remaining deficit in step 5 is smaller, the DG runs at $P_{DG,\;min}$ and the surplus is rectified from AC to DC and stored in the battery or dumped, depending on its SOC (step 3). \\ [1ex]
7 & Any demand still unmet is recorded as lost load and counts toward DPSP. Incentive-based demand response (DR) or demand-side management (DSM), which ask consumers to shift or curtail load, are an alternative to load shedding (\cref{sec:dr}). \\ [1ex]
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Load-following rule used in the sizing simulation.}
\label{tab:dispatch_strat_for_sizing} """)
E.rep(r"Ramp rate limits on the DG & This determines how quickly it can be ramped up to, or ramped down from the rated power. However, NERC (North American Electric Reliability Corporation) disturbance control standards specify that regulating and supplemental reserves must be able to reach their full name-plate capacity within 10-20 minutes \cite{nerc}. Thus, it did not make sense to try and enforce this constraint in the current model since an hourly time step is used. \\ [1ex]",
      r"Ramp rate limits on the DG & Not enforced: NERC (North American Electric Reliability Corporation) disturbance control standards require regulating and supplemental reserves to reach full nameplate capacity within 10--20 minutes \cite{nerc}, well within the hourly time step. \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"Power limits of the DG & Whenever the DG is online (i.e., when $P_{DG}(t) > 0$), its power output must lie between the rated minimum and maximum powers specified. \\ [1ex]",
      r"Power limits of the DG & When the DG is online ($P_{DG}(t) > 0$), its output lies between its minimum stable output and its rated power. \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"Positivity constraints & The power output of the DG must be non-negative at all times. \\ [1ex]", r"Positivity & The DG output is non-negative. \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"Power limits on the BS & Both the charging (-ve) and discharging (+ve) powers of the BS must not exceed its maximum power rating. \\ [1ex]",
      r"Power limits on the BS & Charging (negative) and discharging (positive) battery power do not exceed the power rating. \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"Safe battery operation & Upper and lower limits on the $SOC$ of the BS. \\ [1ex]", r"Safe battery operation & The SOC stays between $SOC_{min}$ and $SOC_{max}$. \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"Reliability requirement & Upper limit on the DPSP for the MG to be reliable and ensure power balance. \\ [1ex]", r"Reliability requirement & DPSP does not exceed an upper limit (dispatch problem). \\ [1ex]")
E.rep(r"\caption{Summary of MG constraints.}", r"\caption{Constraints of the sizing and dispatch problems.}")
para(r"The main constraints in this problem are listed in \cref{tab:dispatch_constraints}.",
     r"""\cref{tab:dispatch_constraints} lists the constraints. The weighted sizing problem is:""")
para(r"where all the weights sum up to unity i.e., $w_1 + w_2 + w_3 + w_4 + w_5 = 1$",
     r"""where the weights sum to one ($w_1 + w_2 + w_3 + w_4 + w_5 = 1$) and $n_s, \; n_w, \; E_{b,\; init} \geq 0$. The remaining constraints are enforced inside the MATLAB simulation that evaluates the objective, through the dispatch rule of \cref{sec:disp_strat}.""")
para(r"Similarly, the optimization problem for day-ahead dispatch can be written as:", r"The day-ahead dispatch problem is:")
para(r"where $\vv{\bm{P_{DG}}}$ and $\vv{\bm{P_{BS}}}$ are $1\times24$ row vectors",
r"""where $\vv{\bm{P_{DG}}}$ and $\vv{\bm{P_{BS}}}$ are $1\times24$ vectors of hourly DG and battery power, and $\vv{\bm{SOC}}$ is a $1\times25$ vector whose last element, $SOC(25)$, keeps the state of charge within limits at the end of the day. $DPSP_{max}$ sets the required reliability as the largest allowed share of daily unmet load. Because each objective is normalized (\cref{app:costs}), the objective values are dimensionless: DPSP, dump and $1-REF$ lie between 0 and 1, while normalized LCOE and emissions exceed 1 only for a design that costs more or emits more than the diesel-only baseline. By default both problems weight the objectives equally: $w_i = 0.2$ in sizing and $w_i = 0.25$ in dispatch.""")
para(r"A convex optimization problem is one with a convex objective as well as all convex constraints.",
r"""Both problems have linear, and hence convex, constraints, but their objectives are nonconvex, nonlinear and non-smooth: discrete states such as the DG status $DG_{ON}$ and the battery cycle count $BS_{cycled}$ make them discontinuous and non-differentiable, and several component sub-models are nonlinear. The numbers of PV modules $n_s$ and wind turbines $n_w$ are integers. Our sizing runs search an integer-coded space: the solvers move in the continuous box, the objective rounds $n_s$ and $n_w$ to the nearest integers before simulating, so every evaluated design can be built, and a final one-dimensional search re-optimizes $E_{b,\;init}$ for the rounded design. \cref{sec:results} also reports the alternative relax-and-round strategy, in which the solvers optimize the continuous relaxation and only the returned point is rounded. Mixed-integer formulations of such problems are NP-hard \cite{parisio2013}. The number of variables and constraints of the dispatch problem grows with the horizon and with finer time steps, so we restrict dispatch to one day ahead in hourly steps. Both problems are solved with MATLAB's Optimization and Global Optimization Toolboxes.""")

