para(r"The renewable power generation sector today is heavily dependent on",
r"""Renewable power in the region relies heavily on biomass and large hydropower, which we exclude. Biomass such as firewood and charcoal is carbon-neutral but harms vegetation and biodiversity, is land-intensive, drives deforestation and competes with food crops. Large hydroelectric schemes damage local ecosystems and displace communities, most well-suited sites in East African countries such as Kenya and Tanzania are already developed, and recurring droughts reduce reservoir head and flow and hence the hydropower available \cite{usea}. Wind turbines and solar PV are therefore the only renewables considered.""")
E.rep(r"Maximum power point tracking (MPPT) via the power conditioner is also used to extract maximum possible power from the system. The power conditioning efficiency $\eta_{pc}= 1$ since it is assumed that a perfect MPPT controller is used \cite{kaabeche2011b}. This system used Mitsubishi Electric PV modules with the parameters shown in \cref{tab:pv_cell}. These are monocrystalline Silicon cells with a power rating of 255 $W$ each. ",
      r"The power conditioner performs maximum power point tracking (MPPT); assuming perfect MPPT, the power conditioning efficiency is $\eta_{pc}= 1$ \cite{kaabeche2011b}. We use Mitsubishi Electric monocrystalline silicon modules rated at 255~W (\cref{tab:pv_cell}).")
para(r"A small-scale on-shore horizontal-axis wind turbine system from Raum Energy",
r"""We use small on-shore horizontal-axis turbines from Raum Energy rated at 3.5~kW each (five blades, downwind). A small rating suits the low demand of the rural community and keeps installation and permitting fees, and hence the upfront capital cost, low. \cref{tab:wt_spec} lists the specifications. Turbine output depends on the wind speed at the site, the air density $\rho_{air}$, the swept area $A_{swept}$ and the efficiency $\eta_w$ with which the rotor converts the kinetic energy of the wind into electricity \cite{tazvinga2014}:""")
E.rep(r"v_{hub}(t) = v_{ref}(t) \; \left(\frac{h_{hub}}{h_{ref}}\right)^\beta", r"v_{hub}(t) = v_{ref}(t) \; \left(\frac{h_{hub}}{h_{ref}}\right)^{\alpha_w}")
para(r"\noindent where $n_w$ is the total number of wind turbines and $v_{hub}$ is the wind speed at the rotor hub",
r"""\noindent where $n_w$ is the number of turbines and $v_{hub}$ is the wind speed at hub height $h_{hub}$, extrapolated from the hourly wind speed $v_{ref}$ measured at the reference height $h_{ref}$ close to the ground. The power-law exponent $\alpha_w$ ranges from below 0.1 over flat land, water or ice to above 0.25 over forest \cite{kaabeche2011a}; we use 0.14, which suits open grassland away from tall trees and buildings, as around rural villages in Kenya and Tanzania \cite{kaviani2009}. A variable-speed permanent-magnet generator keeps the rotor at the tip-speed ratio $\lambda$ that maximizes the power coefficient $C_p$ and the conversion efficiency $\eta_w$, and hence the power output \cite{seeling1997}.""")
