para(r"In order to ensure system reliability, standby distributed generators using conventional fossil fuels",
r"""Standby fossil-fueled generators supply the demand that renewables and storage cannot meet, for example after sudden increases in demand or during poor weather. As for the wind turbines, we assume variable-speed operation, which uses less fuel at part load than constant-speed operation because the engine or turbine slows down at low demand \cite{seeling1997}. To keep the problem simple, the system has a single DG. In the sizing problem the rating of the diesel engine or microturbine is fixed; \cref{sec:pareto_rel} treats the DE rating as a decision variable. The rating covers the peak demand with a margin, $P_{DG,\;r} = P_{load,\;peak} \cdot (1 + SF)$ \cite{ghasemi2013}, where the safety factor $SF$ sets the degree of oversizing; the margin acts as spinning reserve.""")
E.rep(r"These are traditional reciprocating, internal combustion engines based on compression ignition that use diesel as their fuel. These offer several advantages including their dispatchable nature (unlike RES), quick startup and load-following ability but also have demerits such as high fuel costs, pollutant emissions including greenhouse gases and particulates and noise generation \cite{mg_review}. The load peaks at 12.52~kW on the representative day used for dispatch and at 15.0~kW over the year (26 June, 11:00).",
      r"Diesel engines are reciprocating compression-ignition engines. Unlike the RES they are dispatchable, start quickly and follow load well, but they have high fuel costs and emit greenhouse gases, particulates and noise \cite{mg_review}. The load peaks at 12.52~kW on the representative day used for dispatch and at 15.0~kW over the year (8 June, 10:00--11:00).")
para(r"Another option for the backup DG is a microturbine, which can run on a diverse range",
r"""A microturbine can run on a wide range of gaseous and liquid fuels and suits combined heat and power (CHP) applications, for which natural gas is the most common fuel. Microturbines emit less greenhouse gas than DEs, and much less than large central natural gas plants, especially $NO_x$ and $CO_2$ \cite{wu2014}, but they cost more upfront, often have higher fuel costs than diesel, and are far less common in Africa than diesel gensets. They usually range from 30 to 330~kW; for a direct comparison we use the same rating as the DE, $P_{MT,\; r} = P_{MT, \; max} = 16$~kW with $P_{MT,\;min} = 4.8$~kW. A 61~kW MT burns 0.84~MMBtu per hour at rated power \cite{chp_mt}; scaling this, the hourly fuel use [MMBtu/h] at output $P_{MT}$ is $Fuel_{MT}(t) = \frac{0.84}{61} \cdot P_{MT}(t)$, and at a natural gas price of $C_{NG} = 2.19$~USD/MMBtu \cite{ng_price} the hourly fuel cost is $C_{fuel,\;MT}(t) = 2.19 \cdot Fuel_{MT}(t)$.""")
para(r"The battery's state of charge must remain within the upper and lower limits",
r"""The SOC must stay between $SOC_{min}$ and $SOC_{max}$. The simulation starts with the battery at $SOC_{max} = 90\%$ for both LI and LA. The lower limit follows from the depth of discharge $DoD$, the deepest level to which the battery can be safely discharged: $E_{b,\;min} = (1 - DoD)\cdot E_C$. The maximum capacity $E_C$ starts at the rated capacity $E_{b,\; init}$ and fades linearly with cycling, $E_C = E_{b,\; init} \cdot (1 - n_{cycles}\cdot \Delta_E)$, where $n_{cycles}$ is the cumulative number of equivalent full cycles, computed as the energy discharged divided by the usable capacity $(SOC_{max}-SOC_{min})\,E_{b,\;init}$ and updated every time step; over the one-year simulation this gives the number of cycles per year. The fade rates in \cref{tab:bs_params} assume that the battery is replaced when $E_C$ falls to 70\% of its rated value.""")
para(r"Some of the Li-ion parameters were updated using the latest values for the Tesla Powerwall",
r"""Some Li-ion parameters follow the Tesla Powerwall \cite{powerwall}, assuming that the battery can be fully cycled daily for five years beyond its 10-year warranty \cite{bs_source}.""")
para(r"\cref{tab:bs_params} clearly shows trade-offs in terms of the relative advantages",
r"""Lead-acid batteries have shorter lifetimes, requiring more frequent replacement, and lower round-trip efficiency than Li-ion (\cref{tab:bs_params}). Li-ion also allows deeper discharge, so more of its capacity is usable, and its higher power rating per kWh lets it serve more of the demand. It has slightly higher self-discharge, mainly from its protection circuit, and costs more per kWh, but its slower capacity fade means fewer replacements, which can make it cheaper over the project life, depending on how often it is cycled.""")

# =========================================================== Appendix B
para(r"Since this problem involves a multi-period optimization over the entire project lifecycle",
     r"""The project lasts $t_{sys} = 25$~y, so all cash flows are discounted to the present; \cref{tab:financial} lists the financial assumptions.""")
para(r"The initial capital (IC) investment for the RES sources (PV and WT) and backup DGs",
r"""Initial capital costs (IC) of the PV, WT and DGs scale with rated power and those of the batteries with rated energy capacity. Replacement costs (RC) are a fraction of IC because they exclude one-time permitting and installation fees. PV, WT and batteries have only fixed annual operation and maintenance (O\&M) costs, set as a fraction of IC, and the converter has none. The DGs also incur fuel costs, variable O\&M per operating hour, and startup ($SUC$) and shutdown ($SDC$) costs. \cref{tab:costs1,tab:costs2} summarize the values.""")
