para(r"The cost calculations for day-ahead dispatch are similar to those for sizing",
     r"""Daily dispatch costs cover only the operating costs incurred over the 24~h horizon, such as fuel and O\&M, and exclude capital and replacement costs, so no discounting is needed:""")
para(r"While optimizing the design, sizing and dispatch of the MG, there are several objectives",
     r"""Sizing and dispatch pursue several objectives that can conflict, so we solve a multiobjective, multiperiod, constrained and bounded problem, which also exposes the trade-offs between the objectives. The five objectives are defined below.""")
para(r"$LCOE$ [$\$/kWh$] is the ratio of total annual costs", r"""$LCOE$ [USD/kWh] is the total annualized cost divided by the annual energy served:""")
para(r"where $\Sigma_{t=1}^{t=8760} \; E_{load}(t)$ is the total electrical energy",
     r"""where $\Sigma_{t=1}^{t=8760} \; E_{load}(t)$ is the annual energy consumed [kWh] with hourly steps ($\Delta t = 1$~h). Sizing minimizes LCOE to keep electricity affordable; dispatch minimizes the daily cost of electricity (COE) [USD/kWh] instead:""")
para(r"In order to mitigate negative environmental impacts of the MG, it is necessary to minimize emissions",
     r"""The emissions objective covers the airborne pollutants emitted by the backup DGs, including particulate matter (PM), volatile organic compounds (VOC) and greenhouse gases (GHG). \cref{tab:emissions} lists the emission factors for diesel in the DE and natural gas in the MT. Total emissions $Em$ [kg] are the sum of these factors multiplied by the energy from fossil sources, $\Sigma_{t=1}^{t=T} \; P_{DG}(t) \cdot \Delta t$, with $T = 24$~h for dispatch and 8,760~h for sizing.""")
para(r"In order to put $LCOE$ and emissions on a scale comparable to the other three objectives",
     r"""To put LCOE and emissions on a scale comparable to the other three objectives, which lie between 0 and 1, we divide them by the LCOE and emissions of a baseline in which a single DG (a DE or an MT, matching the hybrid system) with rating $P_{DG,\;r} = P_{load,\; peak} \cdot (1+SF)$, $SF \geq 0$, supplies the whole load. The baseline DG runs throughout the study period with one startup and one shutdown, and needs no converter because its AC output supplies the load directly. Its costs, used to compute $LCOE_{base}$, are:""")
E.rep(r"\noindent where $OM_{var, \; DG,\; annual}$ and $C_{fuel,\; DG,\; annual}$ are calculated assuming the DG is run for 8760 h to meet the entire annual load. Similarly, $C_{fuel,\; DG,\; daily}$ and $OM_{var, \; DG,\; daily}$ are calculated assuming the DG is run for 24 h to meet the entire daily load. The base emissions $Em_{base}$ are also calculated assuming that the DG meets all the power demand, on a daily and annual basis.",
      r"\noindent where $OM_{var, \; DG,\; annual}$ and $C_{fuel,\; DG,\; annual}$ assume that the DG runs for 8,760~h to meet the annual load, and $C_{fuel,\; DG,\; daily}$ and $OM_{var, \; DG,\; daily}$ that it runs for 24~h to meet the daily load. $Em_{base}$ likewise assumes that the DG meets all demand.")
para(r"$DPSP$ is the ratio between the total unmet or deficit load",
     r"""$DPSP$ is the ratio of unmet load, i.e.\ consumption that is curtailed, interrupted or disrupted, to the total demand over the study period \cite{prasad2006}:""")
para(r"where $T = 8760\;h$ for sizing and $T = 24\;h$ for dispatch. Thus, $0 \leq DPSP \leq 1$",
     r"""where $T = 8{,}760$~h for sizing and $T = 24$~h for dispatch. DPSP lies between 0 (all load served) and 1 (no load served); minimizing it maximizes reliability.""")
para(r"This is the ratio between excess power (DC) generated by the system",
     r"""REPG is the ratio of the excess DC power sent to dump loads or grounded to the total DC power generated \cite{kaabeche2011b}:""")
para(r"where \cref{eq:Pbal} arises from the power balance in the system.",
     r"""where the second equality in \cref{eq:Pbal} follows from the power balance. REPG measures how efficiently the system uses its generation; minimizing it ($0 \leq REPG \leq 1$) reduces wasted energy and renewable curtailment.""")
para(r"The renewable energy fraction (REF) measures the proportion of total power generation",
     r"""The renewable energy fraction (REF) is the share of generation from renewable sources:""")
para(r"where $T$ is either 8760 h or 24 h for sizing and dispatch, respectively. In order to improve the sustainability",
     r"""where $T$ is 8,760~h for sizing and 24~h for dispatch. Maximizing REF ($0 \leq REF \leq 1$), or equivalently minimizing $1-REF$, reduces dependence on fossil fuels.""")

