# =========================================================== Appendix D
para(r"The current strategy assumed for sizing optimization results in DGs being used during peak demand",
     r"""The sizing rule starts the DG whenever renewable output and the battery cannot cover the load. Running the DG at times of low demand and reserving battery and renewable energy for peak hours would need a different priority order, in which part of the renewable output charges the battery instead of serving the load at once. Other strategies may improve the objectives further, so the space of dispatch strategies deserves exploration at the design stage.""")
para(r"Non-convex formulations generally entail greater computational complexity",
     r"""Nonconvex formulations are harder and slower to solve. Relaxations, variable transformations or linearizations could make the problem easier; for example, replacing the discrete decision variables by continuous ones could convexify the problem and speed up optimization, and could make room for more detailed nonlinear component models that are intractable in a nonconvex setting. Vectorizing the objective function would also shorten run times.""")
para(r"The multi-period optimization could also be refined to simulate the system more accurately",
     r"""The sizing simulation covers one year and assumes that later years repeat it, with discounting. It therefore ignores the 10--15\% annual load growth expected in Kenya and Tanzania from population growth, economic development and rising incomes and living standards \cite{usea}, as well as year-to-year changes in other parameters, such as volatile fuel prices, which the sensitivity analysis covers only one at a time. Dispatch is optimized one day at a time; longer horizons would show how the system responds to multi-day disturbances.""")
para(r"Discretizing into finer time steps in the order of minutes",
     r"""Sub-hourly steps (e.g., $\Delta t = 5$~min) would represent the battery dynamics and SOC more accurately and allow DG ramp-rate limits to be enforced on startup and shutdown. They would also smooth the step changes in the hourly dispatch and SOC profiles. The cost is more computation and the need for sub-hourly climate and load data, which are rarely available for rural African sites.""")
para(r"The system's robustness to the stochasticity of demand and load fluctuations can be improved",
     r"""Demand-side management can make the system more robust to load fluctuations. Demand response (DR) programs have been modeled with game theory, mechanism design and auction theory. In one such model \cite{nwulu2017}, the microgrid operator maximizes its net expected benefit:""")
para(r"where $x$ is the amount of power consumption that is shifted or curbed by a customer of type",
     r"""where $x$ is the load shifted or curtailed by a customer of type $0 \leq \theta \leq 1$, which measures the customer's willingness to shift or curb demand ($\theta = 1$ for the most willing and $\theta = 0$ for the least willing). $\lambda$ is the value of power interruptibility, which measures how much the operator gains from modifying a specific load and is obtained from an optimal power flow (OPF), and $y$ is the compensation paid to the customer for the load shifted or curtailed. The customer's benefit function is:""")
para(r"where $c(\theta,x)$ is the cost incurred by a customer of type $\theta$",
     r"""where $c(\theta,x)$ is the cost to a customer of type $\theta$ of shifting or reducing load by $x$~kW, increasing in $x$ and decreasing in $\theta$. Constraints cap the operator's compensation budget and each customer's daily interruptible power.""")
para(r"Such agent-based formulations can be useful for simulating behavior in small networks",
     r"""Agent-based formulations of this kind suit small networks such as microgrids, with few market participants, but their computational cost grows quickly with the number of agents and the complexity of the network. They also need two-way communication between all agents, which requires infrastructure upgrades, and smart meters and home energy monitors connected to the Internet of Things (IoT) raise privacy concerns; non-intrusive load monitoring (NILM) and disaggregation can provide enough information for load management while preserving privacy.""")
para(r"The results of such models are also non-deterministic since they involve decision-making",
     r"""The results of such models are also uncertain, because they depend on decisions by consumers, producers and prosumers, who may act irrationally. Each agent's response function, preferences and private costs must be estimated; in the model above, the types $\theta_i$ and cost functions $c_i(\theta_i,x)$ of all participants are hard to determine. The results can often be stated only in probabilistic terms, a further barrier to practical use.""")
para(r"Another approach to mitigate the MG's sensitivity and effects of uncertainty is to build forecasting models",
     r"""More accurate forecasts of load and of solar and wind output would reduce the effect of uncertainty, especially when combined with predictive control and online optimization that update the forecasts at each time step instead of relying on static profiles or historical data. Sizing and dispatch could also be tested with load profiles from sources other than REopt, such as empirical relations for the demand of isolated rural African villages \cite{load_profile}:""")
para(r"The current model assumes perfect foresight and no errors in forecasts.",
     r"""The dispatch model assumes perfect foresight: the scheduler knows the actual demand and renewable output over the whole planning horizon from precalculated load profiles and historical climate data. This is useful for sizing, for designing nominal dispatch strategies and as an idealized benchmark for other strategies \cite{parisio2014}, but such reliable forecasts are rarely available in practice. Model predictive or receding-horizon control (\cref{app:control}) can improve operation under uncertainty. Our objective is a nonconvex, non-smooth black-box function without a closed form or gradient, which makes it harder to cast in standard MPC form; commercial solvers such as GUROBI, however, solve mixed-integer linear and quadratic MPC problems efficiently without complicated heuristics or decomposition \cite{parisio2014}.""")

# =========================================================== Appendix E
para(r"In addition to using available tools like HOMER Pro and DER-CAM, many studies have built their own",
     r"""Besides using tools such as HOMER Pro and DER-CAM, many studies build their own sizing and dispatch models, usually as nonlinear mixed-integer programs. Relaxations and variable transformations can turn these into mixed-integer linear programs, which are more tractable than quadratic programs, and assumptions can be adjusted to convexify the problem; for example, quadratic DG fuel-cost functions are often approximated by the maximum of affine functions \cite{parisio2014}. The subsections below describe common global optimization methods for such problems. These artificial-intelligence search techniques tend to outperform classical methods such as linear programming as dispatch problems grow more complex \cite{Qu2018}; Lyapunov optimization \cite{pathak2017} and discrete Fourier transforms \cite{dft} have also been used.""")
para(r"These are a class of optimization algorithms capable of solving smooth or non-smooth optimization problems",
     r"""Genetic algorithms solve smooth or non-smooth problems with any type of constraint, including integrality. They are stochastic, population-based methods that search the space of candidate solutions by \textit{selection}, \textit{mutation} and \textit{crossover} among population members, imitating evolution by natural selection, with each member representing a candidate solution \cite{nemati2015}. The population of chromosomes is initialized as vectors of bits or character strings. The algorithm minimizes a fitness value that measures deviation from the objectives and constraints, for example a weighted sum of the cost, penalties on constraint violations and a parity degree that measures the similarity among individuals (\cref{eq:GA_app}), where $W_{Pen}$ and $W_{Par}$ are weighting factors and $R_{Cost/Pen}$ and $R_{Cost/Par}$ are ratios of mean values that make the three terms comparable. The parity term rewards diversity: it raises the survival probability of individuals that differ from the group and so reduces the risk of converging to, and stalling at, a local optimum \cite{nemati2015}.""")
para(r"In accordance with Charles Darwin's theory of `survival of the fittest'",
     r"""At each iteration the fitness of every chromosome is evaluated and the best solutions are selected \cite{pathak2017}; these are modified and recombined over many generations until a stopping criterion such as a generation limit is met \cite{nemati2015}. Evolutionary programming rests on the same Darwinian principles, but where GA simulates micro-level processes such as chromosomes, evolutionary algorithms mimic macro-level processes such as phenotype variation and inheritance \cite{pathak2017}. Because they evolve a population, these methods can find multiple well-distributed non-dominated solutions in a single run with little information about the search space \cite{Qu2018}. \textit{Differential evolution} is a variant that differs in the selection step, using non-domination sorting and ranking \cite{Qu2018}; it has solved environmental/economic dispatch (EED) and unit commitment (UC) problems effectively \cite{lu2011}.""")
para(r"One approach for multiobjective optimization is to combine the different individual objective functions",
     r"""Multiobjective problems can be solved by combining the objectives into a weighted sum or by approximating the Pareto set, the set of feasible non-dominated solutions that cannot be improved in one objective without worsening another. Genetic and evolutionary methods are well suited to generating such sets and to showing the trade-offs between objectives such as cost, efficiency and reliability \cite{fadaee2012}.""")
para(r"The main disadvantage with these methods is that complexity rises significantly",
     r"""Their main disadvantage is that complexity, and with it the difficulty of programming and solving, grows quickly with the number of parameters \cite{fathima2015}. Standard GA explores new regions of the search space only to a limited extent, and its crossover and mutation operators, preset from experience, adapt poorly; improved adaptive genetic algorithms avoid premature local convergence and reach global optima faster and more reliably \cite{wu2016}.""")
para(r"Particle swarm optimization is another biologically inspired algorithm",
     r"""Particle swarm optimization (PSO) mimics the collective behavior of a swarm searching for food within a region \cite{fathima2015}, as seen in animal societies and insect colonies \cite{pathak2017}. It is an iterative, agent-based method in which a population of particles, each a candidate solution, explores the space of feasible solutions \cite{alrashidi2010}. PSO is easy to implement, has few parameters and optimizes well, but it can stall in local optima and has difficulty satisfying equality constraints when the swarm is initialized and updated \cite{wu2014}. Improved multiobjective PSO variants have sized microgrid batteries and solved a range of EED problems, including large non-convex problems such as optimal power flow (OPF) \cite{alrashidi2010}, converging quickly to good solutions at low computational cost \cite{pathak2017}.""")
para(r"The conventional PSO algorithm randomly initializes a particle community",
     r"""Standard PSO initializes the particles at random positions $[x_{i1}, x_{i2}, \dots, x_{id}]$ with velocities $[v_{i1}, v_{i2}, \dots, v_{id}]$, where $i = 1, 2, \dots, n$ indexes the $n$ particles and $d$ is the dimension of the search space. Each particle is drawn toward two points: the best position it has found so far ($pbest$) and the best position found by the whole swarm ($gbest$) \cite{borhanazad2014}. Each iteration:""")
para(r"The velocity is updated as a randomly weighted sum of three terms",
     r"""The velocity update in \cref{eq:pso} is a randomly weighted sum of three terms (inertia, attraction to $pbest$ and attraction to $gbest$), where $v_{i,d}^k$ and $x_{i,d}^k$ are the velocity and position of particle $i$ along dimension $d$ in iteration $k$, $\omega$ is the inertia weight, $c_1$ and $c_2$ are learning factors, $pbest_{i,d}^k$ and $gbest_{i,d}^k$ are the local and global best values of coordinate $d$ in iteration $k$, and $rand_1^k$ and $rand_2^k$ are random numbers drawn uniformly from $(0,1)$ \cite{wu2014}. The updates continue until a termination condition, such as an iteration limit or a target fitness, is met, and $gbest$ is returned as the solution. Related swarm-intelligence methods include ant and bee colony optimization, bacterial foraging and group search optimizers \cite{pathak2017}, all part of the wider family of bio-inspired algorithms for complex multiobjective problems.""")
para(r"This algorithm is useful while working with expensive, time-consuming objective functions",
     r"""Surrogate optimization targets objective functions that are expensive to evaluate. It samples quasirandom points within the bounds, interpolates the true objective at these points with a radial basis function to build a cheaper surrogate \cite{gutmann2001}, and takes the minimizer of the surrogate as an approximation of the true minimizer. It balances two goals: exploring the search space for a global minimum and reaching a good solution in few evaluations. It works best on smooth, continuous objectives, although smoothness is not required, and it converges to the global optimum on bounded domains, though slowly \cite{gutmann2001}. It can greatly speed up sizing and dispatch when each evaluation is expensive; when evaluations are cheap, as in our 8,760-hour simulation, fitting the surrogate dominates the run time (\cref{tab:bench_stats}). No stopping criterion reliably detects that the solver is near a global optimum, so it stops after a set number of iterations, evaluations or time and returns the best point found within that budget.""")
para(r"This is a stochastic search technique modelled after the annealing process in metals",
     r"""Simulated annealing (SA) is a stochastic search modeled on the annealing of metals, which are melted and then cooled slowly into low-energy crystalline structures; the temperature schedule is the key control. At a fixed temperature $T$, the current state is perturbed to a new state with energy difference $\Delta E$. The new state is accepted if $\Delta E < 0$; otherwise it is accepted with probability:""")
para(r"where $k_B$ is the Boltzmann constant. These perturbations are repeated until the system reaches thermal equilibrium",
     r"""where $k_B$ is the Boltzmann constant. Perturbations repeat until the system reaches equilibrium at temperature $T$, after which the temperature is lowered slightly and the sequence repeats. In microgrid optimization the energy $E$ becomes the objective, the temperature $T$ a control parameter, and \cref{eq:sa} the probability of accepting a candidate system configuration or dispatch strategy \cite{fathima2015}. SA is less common than GA, EA and PSO for the sizing and dispatch of hybrid systems; one study found it faster to converge but less efficient than tabu search for sizing that minimizes LCOE \cite{sa_tabu}.""")

# =========================================================== Appendix F
para(r"After completing (1) the initial design to choose and size the generation and storage technologies",
     r"""Beyond design and dispatch scheduling, a third area of research concerns the real-time control of microgrids. Closed-loop control is likely to outperform open-loop, model-based scheduling in practice, because it uses measurements of the actual system state to correct for variations in demand and supply \cite{tazvinga2014}. Control is essential to absorb the uncertainty of intermittent renewable output, and more so as localized, distributed generation grows, especially in islanded and off-grid systems, where power quality, safety and overloads are particular concerns \cite{pourbabak2017}; bidirectional flows of power and information add complexity. Feedback control therefore improves robustness to forecast errors in load and renewable output and to disturbances such as wind lulls and other extreme weather, natural disasters and sudden demand spikes from heating or cooling loads. Microgrid control is conventionally organized in three levels. Primary control regulates the voltage, frequency and power of individual converters. Secondary control improves power quality, mitigating voltage or frequency deviations, imbalances and harmonics; in practice it restores frequency and voltage to their nominal values after primary control by adjusting real and reactive power set-points in real time. Tertiary control handles power exchanges with the main grid or with adjacent microgrids \cite{dragicevic_2017}, and can include system-wide techno-economic optimization at the design stage and efficiency improvements in day-to-day dispatch \cite{mg_review}. Control architectures for large cyber-physical systems such as microgrids are centralized, decentralized or distributed.""")
para(r"This is the most mature and well-researched of the three methods",
     r"""Centralized control is the most mature of the three architectures and has long been used to control and economically dispatch generators in power systems \cite{su2015}. A single controller collects and processes data from the whole system and sends control signals to each agent through supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA), an automation system that centrally manages the control, data gathering and monitoring of a power system \cite{scada}. The framework is conceptually simple and has been studied for decades, producing many controllers, particularly for linear systems \cite{farina2012}. It is hard to implement in practice, however, because it needs extensive two-way communication that grows with the number of agents; real-time transmission of measurements and computation of control actions may be infeasible over a large area, so cost and computation scale poorly with network size, and offline model-based synthesis is difficult for systems with many inputs, states and outputs. It also requires all agents to share private information with the central controller, which may be unacceptable for socioeconomic and political reasons \cite{farina2012}. Centralized control is nevertheless a good starting point for small microgrids with few producers, consumers and prosumers and simple topologies, which an operator can later move to a distributed scheme as the system grows.""")
para(r"This is a schematic where each agent, node, or subsystem",
     r"""In decentralized control, each agent, node or subsystem (e.g., a DG, consumer or battery) has its own controller and acts only on local measurements such as voltage or frequency \cite{pourbabak2017}. No global information is needed, agents keep their information private because they share it neither with a central unit nor with other nodes \cite{gan2012}, control variables are computed in parallel, and real-time communication is easier because sensors, controllers and actuators within a subsystem are close together \cite{farina2012}. Without communication between controllers, however, performance is limited and global optimality or stability cannot be guaranteed \cite{pourbabak2017}. Both centralized and decentralized schemes have improved transient stability in small power systems such as microgrids \cite{senjyu2005}, and decentralized systems can be more robust than comparable centralized ones: if some nodes disconnect because of faults, the others keep operating on local information.""")
para(r"This is a consensus-based approach where individual agents or nodes use both locally measured parameters",
     r"""Distributed control is consensus-based: each agent uses local measurements and information shared with its nearest neighbors through two-way communication. This enables global optimization with efficient parallel computation, lets the designer trade performance against communication burden and adapt the network design \cite{farina2012}, and leaves agents some control over their privacy. Distributed control thus combines strengths of the centralized and decentralized schemes and is increasingly popular for microgrids. Because information is shared locally, it copes with the changing structure of the microgrid and of future smart grids, and it meets the \textit{peer-to-peer} and \textit{plug-and-play} requirements. Peer-to-peer operation means that no single component is critical, so the system keeps working when an individual generator or storage unit fails; plug-and-play means that a new unit can be installed anywhere without reconfiguring the network or controller \cite{mg_prop}. The microgrid can then grow as producers and customers connect \cite{pourbabak2017}, which suits remote rural electrification. Its scalability, fast response and model independence also suit real-time DR and DSM algorithms that engage users and generators \cite{deng2014} and systems with flexible DGs and responsive loads \cite{dist_1, dist_2}. Open challenges are the design of the communication and control topology, infrastructure upgrades for bidirectional flows, and fast convergence to consensus among local agents \cite{pourbabak2017}.""")
para(r"Model predictive control (MPC) is one of the most widely used methods",
     r"""Model predictive control (MPC) is one of the most widely used methods for optimal microgrid operation. It turns the static optimization into a dynamic one by solving, at each sampling instant, an open-loop optimal control problem over a receding finite horizon from the current state; only the first control action is applied, and the process repeats from the new state \cite{mpc}. Linear MPC solves a quadratic (QP) or linear program (LP) at each control interval, i.e.\ a quadratic or linear cost subject to linear constraints. Unlike PI and PID controllers, or control laws synthesized offline as a precomputed feedback policy for all states, MPC solves the optimal control problem online for the \textit{current} state \cite{mpc}. It can therefore anticipate future events, such as fluctuations in renewable supply or changes in demand, and act to damp their effects while satisfying time-varying operating constraints \cite{parisio2014}, which improves performance over periods in which disturbances occur.""")
para(r"Variants of predictive control implemented in the literature include formulations",
     r"""Predictive control has been implemented with mixed-integer linear and nonlinear programs, because microgrid models need both discrete variables (e.g., the on/off states of DGs) and continuous ones (e.g., battery SOC and charge and discharge rates) \cite{parisio2013, parisio2014}. Such feedback compensates for uncertain renewable output and time-varying loads. Robust linear and nonlinear economic MPC (E-MPC) methods optimize dispatch set-points to minimize operating cost \cite{zachar2016} or to maximize profit from selling power to the main grid \cite{pereira2017}; finite-horizon MPC objectives comprise stage, terminal and penalty costs, and E-MPC uses economic costs as the stage cost. Closed-loop discrete linear MPC has been applied to a PV--wind--diesel--battery system similar to the one studied here \cite{tazvinga2014}. Predictive control has also handled uncertainty in generator model parameters \cite{prodan2014}, and adaptive, switched MPC uses switching constraints, rather than switched state-space models, to represent transitions such as between battery charging and discharging \cite{zhu2015}.""")

